WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:02.774
Positions versus interests.

2
00:00:03.473 --> 00:00:09.626
The distinction comes from Getting to Yes, the book by Fisher and Ury, from 1981.

3
00:00:10.685 --> 00:00:13.123
A position is what someone says they want.

4
00:00:13.873 --> 00:00:16.439
For example: no stops during the week.

5
00:00:17.458 --> 00:00:18.886
An interest is why.

6
00:00:19.593 --> 00:00:22.763
We can't miss the weekly delivery to our biggest customer.

7
00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:26.298
Positions clash; interests often don't.

8
00:00:27.076 --> 00:00:31.291
Maybe a four-hour stop on Monday morning, when the order has already shipped,

9
00:00:31.488 --> 00:00:32.405
works for both.

10
00:00:33.396 --> 00:00:35.021
So ask: what do you need it for?

11
00:00:35.755 --> 00:00:37.160
And: what are you worried about?

12
00:00:38.518 --> 00:00:40.027
Two people clash in your team?

13
00:00:40.766 --> 00:00:43.297
Then first listen to both sides separately.

14
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.534
Don't take sides.

15
00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:48.305
Not even when one of them is right.

16
00:00:49.392 --> 00:00:52.353
Then bring them together, with the interests on the table.

17
00:00:53.041 --> 00:00:55.270
You both want the line to run reliably.

18
00:00:55.965 --> 00:00:57.915
You disagree on when to stop it.

19
00:00:58.976 --> 00:01:01.101
Look for options that serve both.

20
00:01:02.099 --> 00:01:06.998
And decide on objective criteria: facts both sides accept beforehand,

21
00:01:07.138 --> 00:01:09.785
such as downtime hours or missed deliveries.

22
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.262
Not on who shouts loudest.

23
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:15.433
Know each side's BATNA too,

24
00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:20.425
another term from the same book: the best alternative to a negotiated agreement,

25
00:01:20.517 --> 00:01:22.200
what they do if there is no deal.

26
00:01:23.178 --> 00:01:26.928
Without a shared stop, maintenance's alternative is a breakdown.

27
00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:29.877
And production's is a missed delivery.

28
00:01:30.864 --> 00:01:33.441
Seeing that makes a deal a lot more attractive.

29
00:01:34.426 --> 00:01:36.725
A failure mode: splitting the difference.

30
00:01:37.409 --> 00:01:41.577
Two hours instead of four satisfies nobody, and fixes nothing.

31
00:01:42.621 --> 00:01:46.743
And some conflicts are about values or status, not interests.

32
00:01:47.504 --> 00:01:49.849
Then escalate to someone who can decide.

33
00:01:51.203 --> 00:01:52.143
An example.

34
00:01:52.853 --> 00:01:55.883
You are a junior Lean consultant at a dairy plant.

35
00:01:56.976 --> 00:02:01.387
In your weekly meeting, the production manager and the maintenance manager clash.

36
00:02:02.411 --> 00:02:06.196
Production says: no more stops on line 1, we're behind.

37
00:02:07.253 --> 00:02:10.793
Maintenance says: then it will break down, and that's worse.

38
00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:15.048
You stop the discussion, and talk to each of them alone.

39
00:02:16.074 --> 00:02:20.138
Production's interest: a big retail order ships every Thursday.

40
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:26.409
Maintenance's interest: three hours a week to replace worn seals, before they fail.

41
00:02:27.742 --> 00:02:29.286
When you bring them back together,

42
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.058
you name the shared interest: you both want line 1 reliable for Thursday.

43
00:02:35.041 --> 00:02:37.119
Production fears missing the shipment.

44
00:02:37.894 --> 00:02:40.843
Maintenance fears a breakdown that would also miss it.

45
00:02:41.996 --> 00:02:46.048
Then you put an option on the table: a three-hour stop on Friday morning,

46
00:02:46.211 --> 00:02:48.115
the day after the Thursday shipment.

47
00:02:49.163 --> 00:02:51.636
And you decide on objective criteria.

48
00:02:52.408 --> 00:02:57.505
Agree to try it for four weeks, then look at downtime hours and missed deliveries.

49
00:02:58.562 --> 00:03:00.373
Not at who argued loudest.

50
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.446
Then the difficult conversation: the talk you would rather avoid.

51
00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:07.587
Do it in four steps.

52
00:03:08.595 --> 00:03:10.627
First, what you saw, factually.

53
00:03:11.289 --> 00:03:14.946
For example: twice this month the data wasn't there on Monday.

54
00:03:15.994 --> 00:03:19.883
Then the impact: the planning meeting then decides on guesses.

55
00:03:20.881 --> 00:03:22.738
Ask for their view, and listen.

56
00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.314
How do you see it?

57
00:03:25.382 --> 00:03:27.158
Agree the next step together.

58
00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.867
And prepare your first sentence.

59
00:03:30.545 --> 00:03:31.660
The rest follows.

60
00:03:32.969 --> 00:03:33.944
And with the client?

61
00:03:34.718 --> 00:03:38.885
When a manager blames your project, don't defend yourself in the room.

62
00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:43.045
Say instead: I hear you're unhappy with how this is going.

63
00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:46.612
Can we take twenty minutes tomorrow to go through it?

64
00:03:47.674 --> 00:03:50.274
In that talk, use the same four steps.

65
00:03:51.339 --> 00:03:55.786
When you don't know what to do, separate the people, and talk to each alone.

66
00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:59.295
And ask a senior colleague before you act.
